FREEING THE PARROT 2.0

FTP2.0 — Execution Report

FINAL STATUS: LOCKED PRODUCTION BASELINE

##

Project and purpose FTP2.0 is an interactive “apparatus” that guides a participant through a conversation, analyzes interaction signals, presents a Data Showdown, generates 27 reflective readings, allows card selection, and ends with a closing reflection. It is designed to produce readings, not rewards or diagnoses. The project consists of:

A frontend experience in frontend/kimi.

A Python backend in ftp_backend_service.py and supporting ftp/ modules.

A Navarasa analysis engine.

A session lifecycle, evidence, interaction-profile, reading, card-deck, and reveal pipeline.

Vercel deployment for the frontend.

A Render backend URL configured through VITE_FTP_API_URL.

Neon-related environment variables and persistence support.

##

Original execution flow The intended journey is:

Participant opens FTP2.0.

Participant enters the experience and supplies conversational input.

The backend creates a session.

The parrot conducts the conversation.

Interaction evidence and analysis are collected.

The Navarasa engine analyzes emotional signals.

The Data Showdown presents the collected interaction information.

The participant reaches the 27-card reading deck.

Cards can be flipped, selected, shuffled, and reviewed.

The final reveal and closing stage are shown.

The participant can begin again.

##

Failure states encountered

A. Backend unavailable / “Failed to fetch” The browser displayed:

The apparatus could not reach its backend. Please try again. The frontend was unable to complete requests to the backend. The configured backend variable was later confirmed as: `https\://ftp2-backend.onrender.com` The backend’s optional persistence layer could also fail while the live session was still otherwise usable.

B. Vercel 404 The public site displayed:

404 NOT_FOUND The cause was a deployment-output mismatch. The frontend built into `kimi_dist`, but Vercel was configured to look for `dist`. The live site therefore had no published entry page even though source files existed. A second deployment problem was found: Vercel installed root dependencies but did not install the frontend’s separate dependencies. The frontend needed packages such as Vite and its React plugin. The root build command was updated to install `frontend/kimi` dependencies before building. Another important deployment issue was branch mismatch: the live deployment was still using an older `FTP_2.0` commit while fixes existed on separate `v0/...` branches. A successful build on a separate branch did not automatically mean the production URL had received the fix.

C. Lifecycle 404 / unknown POST route The backend correctly advanced state for input_complete and card_selection, but those branches did not return a success response. Execution continued into the generic unknown-route response, producing a 404 even though the action itself had succeeded. This was fixed by returning JSON success responses immediately after both lifecycle actions.

D. Card spoiler validation failure The frontend displayed:

Internal FTP error: participant-facing card text contains analytical spoilers The reading seed sent into the participant-facing deck could contain internal analytical terms, IDs, or model language. Examples included evidence IDs, inference IDs, confidence, telemetry, Gemini, Navarasa labels, and analysis wording. The deck validator correctly rejected this text rather than showing internal system information to the participant. The sanitizer was strengthened so contaminated seeds are replaced with safe qualitative language while the original analytical information remains available internally as provenance.

E. “NO RASA DETECTED” during engaged conversations The Data Showdown showed no rasa even when the participant was clearly engaged. The cause was an overly narrow Navarasa dictionary. The engine recognized only exact emotional words and phrases. Natural engagement such as “tell me more,” “I want to understand,” “I am interested,” and “this matters to me” was treated as ordinary text. The system then fell back to Shanta with no_emotion_detected. Engagement and curiosity signals were added to the existing Adbhuta category.

F. Auto-commit and push failure The system reported:

There was an error auto committing and pushing changes: Unknown error. The local work was preserved in commit `f31966e` on `v0/session-archetype`. Manual Git pushes were attempted twice, but GitHub returned a server-side Internal Server Error. This was a GitHub/push-service failure, not a code failure. No reset, revert, or deletion was performed.

##

Changes made during the last two days

Reliability and persistence protection

Made optional artifact persistence best-effort.

If database persistence temporarily fails, the live participant session can continue in memory instead of immediately failing.

Made session creation fall back safely when optional persistence is unavailable.

Improved the frontend message for server errors so a temporary service problem is clearer. Commit: a844d93 on v0/prevent-ftp-runtime-failures.

Reading seed privacy and spoiler protection

Added sanitization for participant-facing reading seeds.

Added detection for internal identifiers and analytical terms.

Added safe fallback prose by category.

Preserved original claims, interpretations, confidence, and evidence references internally.

Added regression tests for confidence, Gemini, evidence IDs, Navarasa, telemetry, and safe qualitative text. Commits included:

18462f7 on v0/sanitize-reading-seeds.

3380287 on v0/harden-reading-seed-sanitizer.

Vercel deployment fixes

Corrected Vercel’s output directory from dist to kimi_dist.

Updated the root build to install frontend dependencies before building.

Confirmed local production builds could create the expected output. Commits included:

be8f8b9 on v0/restore-public-project.

3954acc on v0/fix-vercel-frontend-install.

Lifecycle response fix

Added explicit successful JSON responses for input_complete.

Added explicit successful JSON responses for card_selection.

Prevented successful actions from falling through to the generic 404 response. Commit: e54c390.

Requested UI changes Two targeted UI changes were made:

Added a short four-line FTP2.0 explanation above the “BEGIN AGAIN” button.

Reduced the title size of the cards in the 27-card grid. Validation at that point included:

Frontend production build passed.

60 backend regression tests passed.

Live backend health check returned HTTP

200.

Browser smoke check loaded the homepage without browser errors. Commit: c4e5db9 on v0/ftp2-about-and-card-scale.

Navarasa engagement fix

Added engagement, interest, curiosity, excitement, and exploratory phrases to the Navarasa engine.

Added phrases such as “tell me more,” “keep going,” “I want to understand,” “I want to know,” and “this matters to me.”

Verified engaged examples returned Adbhuta rather than no emotion. Commit: 477f766 on v0/fix-navarasa-engagement.

Session archetype implementation A session-scoped archetype was added from the interaction profile, selection pattern, and trajectory. Possible results include:

The Curious Cartographer.

The Pattern Keeper.

The Inner Witness.

The Quiet Observer. The result is explicitly described as applying to the single interaction, not as a permanent psychological label. It is returned by the backend and displayed in the Data Journey. Local commit: f31966e on v0/session-archetype.

